WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.340
Privilege reduction is the practice of removing authority after the program has completed the work that required it.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:12.440
In many designs, elevated rights are needed only for startup or for one narrow operation.

3
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:18.460
After that point, continuing to run with the same authority increases the impact of ordinary defects.

4
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.480
A safer design reduces rights as soon as the work permits.

5
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.480
The exact mechanism depends on the platform.

6
00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:31.180
A POSIX-style program may change identity, adjust group membership, or reduce capabilities.

7
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:41.280
A Windows-style program may use a lower-privilege service identity, adjust token behavior, limit handle inheritance, or split privileged setup into a different component.

8
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.420
The course is not about memorizing one API.

9
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.200
It is about designing the transition and checking that it happened.

10
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.840
Every privilege-change operation must be checked.

11
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:59.700
A failed call, a partial change, or an unexpected platform behavior can leave the process with more authority than the surrounding code assumes.

12
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:09.480
Error handling should be conservative: if the program cannot reduce authority as required by the design, it should avoid continuing into ordinary processing as though nothing happened.

13
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.400
Verification should follow the attempted change.

14
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:22.660
The program can check its resulting identity, group state, capabilities, token properties, accessible resource behavior, or other platform-appropriate indicators.

15
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:27.640
Tests should cover both the successful transition and the failure path.

16
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:33.500
A test that only exercises the happy path may miss the most important behavior.

17
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.780
Document the point of no return.

18
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:41.000
Once privileges are reduced, later code should not rely on privileged resources appearing again.

19
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:48.940
Comments, design notes, function boundaries, and tests should make it clear when elevated authority is expected to be unavailable.

20
00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.240
That clarity helps reviewers spot accidental dependencies and makes future maintenance safer.
